NNAAMMEE
     yypp__mmkkddbb — generate the NIS databases

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     yypp__mmkkddbb --cc
     yypp__mmkkddbb --uu _d_b_n_a_m_e
     yypp__mmkkddbb [--cc] [--bb] [--ss] [--ff] [--ii _i_n_p_u_t_f_i_l_e] [--oo _o_u_t_p_u_t_f_i_l_e]
             [--dd _d_o_m_a_i_n_n_a_m_e] [--mm _m_a_s_t_e_r_n_a_m_e] _i_n_p_u_t_f_i_l_e _d_b_n_a_m_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The yypp__mmkkddbb utility creates db(3) style databases for use with FreeBSD's
     NIS server.  The yypp__mmkkddbb utility reads data from _i_n_p_u_t_f_i_l_e, and writes it
     to _d_b_n_a_m_e in db(3) format (using the hash table method).  The input
     should be in 'key data' format, which is to say two fields of ASCII data
     separated by white space.  The first field is assumed to be the key, and
     everything else is assumed to be the data.  These databases are typically
     stored in _/_v_a_r_/_y_p_/_[_d_o_m_a_i_n_n_a_m_e_] where _d_o_m_a_i_n_n_a_m_e is the name of the NIS
     domain being served.  The yypp__mmkkddbb utility is usually invoked by
     _/_v_a_r_/_y_p_/_M_a_k_e_f_i_l_e.  The yypp__mmkkddbb utility can also be used to dump an NIS
     database file so that its contents can be examined.  For security rea‐
     sons, all databases that yypp__mmkkddbb creates are readable and writable by
     owner only (and usually the owner is root).

     The following options are available:

     --cc      Cause yypp__mmkkddbb to send a YPPROC_CLEAR request to ypserv(8) on the
             local host.  This signal tells the server to close any open data‐
             base descriptors and flush out its database cache.  If used
             alone, this flag signals the server and does nothing else.  If
             used as part of a database creation command, yypp__mmkkddbb will send
             the signal only after the new database has been successfully cre‐
             ated.

     --bb      Cause yypp__mmkkddbb to add a special entry to the database with a key
             of _Y_P___I_N_T_E_R_D_O_M_A_I_N and an empty data field.  If this key is
             present in a map, it alters the behavior of the 'match' procedure
             in ypserv(8) slightly.  If a match query fails (because the
             server could not find a record that matched the supplied key),
             and the _Y_P___I_N_T_E_R_D_O_M_A_I_N key exists within the queried map,
             ypserv(8) will try to match the entry again using a DNS lookup.
             Note that this special behavior only applies to the _h_o_s_t_s maps.
             Using the --bb flag for other maps has no effect.

     --ss      This flag is used to add a special entry to the database with a
             key of _Y_P___S_E_C_U_R_E and an empty data field.  If this key is present
             in a map, ypserv(8) will deny access to the map to any client
             that is not using a reserved port for its query.  This is used
             mainly for the _m_a_s_t_e_r_._p_a_s_s_w_d maps, which should be restricted to
             privileged access only.

     --ff      This flag is used to turn on filtering of lines in the source
             file input that start with ``+'' or ``-'' characters.  These
             characters have special meaning for the _g_r_o_u_p, _p_a_s_s_w_d and
             _m_a_s_t_e_r_._p_a_s_s_w_d maps and hence should not be allowed to appear in
             them as the first character of a key or datum.  If the --ff flag is
             used, yypp__mmkkddbb will reject any source line that starts with a
             ``+'' or ``-'' character and issue a warning message displaying
             the line that was dropped.

     --uu _d_b_n_a_m_e
             Dump (or 'unwind') an NIS database.  This option can be used to
             inspect the contents of an existing NIS database.

     --ii _i_n_p_u_t_f_i_l_e
             When generating an NIS map, encode _i_n_p_u_t_f_i_l_e as a special entry
             in the database with a key of _Y_P___I_N_P_U_T___F_I_L_E.

     --oo _o_u_t_p_u_t_f_i_l_e
             When generating an NIS map, encode _o_u_t_p_u_t_f_i_l_e as a special entry
             in the database with a key of _Y_P___O_U_T_P_U_T___F_I_L_E.

     --dd _d_o_m_a_i_n_n_a_m_e
             When generating an NIS map, encode _d_o_m_a_i_n_n_a_m_e as a special entry
             in the database with a key of _Y_P___D_O_M_A_I_N___N_A_M_E.

     --mm _m_a_s_t_e_r_n_a_m_e
             When generating an NIS map, encode _m_a_s_t_e_r_n_a_m_e as a special entry
             in the database with a key of _Y_P___M_A_S_T_E_R___N_A_M_E.  This entry in the
             database is frequently used by various NIS utilities to determine
             the name of an NIS master server for a domain.  By default,
             yypp__mmkkddbb assumes that the local host is the NIS master; the --mm op‐
             tion is used to override this default.

